hi I'm max Saltonstall and today I'm  going to talk through some simple things  you can do to secure your workloads and  sensitive data in the cloud while still  keeping it private we're going to talk  about how shielded VMs and confidential  VMs can help you do this let's start  with creating a shielded VM in the  Google Cloud console shielded VMs our  virtual machine instances that are  hardened with a set of easily  configurable security features to ensure  that when your VM boots it's running a  verified boot loader and kernel using  shielded VMs helps protect workloads  from threats like remote attacks  privilege escalation and malicious  insiders and yes everything we do here  could also be done through G cloud and  the command line starting at compute  engine go to VM instances and then  create give it a name then go down to  boot disk and click change choose any  image they're all shielded by default  close that window with select at the  bottom and then go down to the tabs  labeled management security disks etc  and click security you can see the  default shielded VM settings let's  enable secure boot here for extra  protection and then click create to spin  it up turning on secure boot gives us  added protection from boot and kernel  level malware once it's ready click on  the new VM and click on stack driver  logging under logs on the detail page at  the logs page you can filter for just VM  integrity logs by clicking on the all  logs drop-down and choosing shielded  underscore VM underscore integrity now  you can see just the shielded VMFS for  this instance let's dig in further click  into the log for early boot report event  then expand the JSON payload then the  early boot report event inside that you  can confirm the VMS integrity state from  the early boot perspective from the line  showing policy evaluation past his true  early boot encompasses firmware and  other drivers loaded during the boot  process of the VM back to the logs list  open up the log that starts with boot  counter then expand JSON payload then  late boot report event shows you details  about the integrity of the rest of your  kernel drivers once again you can  confirm the boot integrity of the VM  from the line paulo  the evaluation past being true that  shows that the VM passed all eight boot  checks and is running what we expect it  to be running no shenanigans now head  into cloud shell to disable secure boot  so we can test this integrity check Oh  [Music]  first stop the VM with G cloud next  disable secure boot using G cloud update  now restart the VM  and you can see in the console that the  integrity verification has failed by  examining the same logs as before  finally make a new integrity baseline  with G cloud so you can compare future  measurements against the current state  pretend that you already triage this  failure and consider it acceptable with  this new baseline established go check  the integrity state of the shielded VM  again in stackdriver logging make sure  you click load newer logs to get the  latest results looking at the late boot  report event again we can see that the  policy evaluation past is true once  again showing that we have met our  integrity standards the ones we just  lowered of course finally time to look  at these metrics over time via  monitoring open up monitoring and head  to metrics Explorer under find resource  type and metric choose the resource GCE  VM instance and the metric early boot  validation  [Music]  then add a second metric late mood  validation we've got our graph though  it's going to make more sense as a  stacked bar graph looks great now you  can keep track of your integrity over  time with gorgeous colors when our  customers are ready to move their  workloads to the cloud one of the  biggest challenges is securely  processing sensitive data in the cloud  while keeping it private Google Cloud  offers solutions to securely bring  sensitive data to the cloud using  encryption to protect data in transit  for offline or online ingestion but when  we have to actually use the data it's  much tougher to keep it encrypted with  confidential computing now in beta  Google Cloud lets you take data security  a step further by encrypting data while  it's being processed in a Google Data  Center confidential computing  environments can now enable encrypted  data to be processed in memory without  exposing it to the rest of the system  and this can be done without the need to  make any changes in your workload or  code let's take a look at the product  but making a new confidential computing  VM which contains a shielded VM to  perform the computation inside compute  engine create a new VM instance enable  confidential computing service with a  checkbox note what changes are being  made to your VMs with this setting then  click enable you can run any workload  you want your VM is now much safer and  more secure with all the memory  encrypted so your data is safe in use  but what about instance templates and  groups you might ask well we've got you  covered the same confidential computing  option is available for instance  templates and you can either use one of  our pre-made images or your own custom  image that best fits your workload  [Music]  now my template defines a shielded  confidential VM setup and I can go to  instance groups to make a managed  instance group with that template beep  bop Boop we're done with a set of  instances auto-scaling for my needs  secured and ready to work  confidential computing environments can  now enable encrypted data to be  processed in memory without exposing it  to the rest of the system and this can  be done without the need to make any  changes in your applications thanks for  joining me today  enjoy shielded VMs and confidential  computing for your workloads on cloud  and check out the link below to get  started  [Music]
 

